  "live_records": {
    "q-37590": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37590",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1,$ and determine which of the following statements is true.",
      "worked_solution": "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy+3{y}^{2}+2x+10y+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(x+y+1) & 2(x+3y+5)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ x+3y+5=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n2y+4=0\\,⟹\\,y=-2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx+(-2)+1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,-2)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x+3y+5))=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+y+1))=2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x+3y+5))=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(1,-2))=2\\cdot 6-{2}^{2}=8>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,-2)=({f}^{″}(1,-2))_{11}=2>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,-2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
